#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111692} :activity/id #uuid "a1f0ae8e-6f0b-56b6-a410-3e3ca9e6eda7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050577 :topic/id #uuid "5555aaf2-f73f-5c86-8f74-5c76cb5a6f50" :topic/math-academy-id 1451} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111921 :tutorial/content "If a parabola has two roots, the axis of symmetry occurs precisely halfway between the roots of the parabola. Therefore, we can find the axis of symmetry of a parabola by finding the roots and then taking their average value. \n\nSo, if the roots are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ the **average of the roots formula** tells us that the axis of symmetry of the parabola is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's consider the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nWe have the roots $x_1=-2$ and $x_2=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{-2+4}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(x+2)(x-4)$ is $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec08bc1ba75082ac98bb6559b6c0ef8119fbf245f3c266710ce8c6a569fa8a02.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "cd361f4f-6e49-5ced-8872-2849078d909c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9250 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0bf344b4-5c54-585a-ab6e-16e5a8e551f8" :step/math-academy-id 502427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110351 :question/id #uuid "30974188-c734-5e99-a9ae-81d1a53b9105" :question/math-academy-id "e-14854"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42832b65-324f-56f7-9cbe-c9bf43389fde" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541116}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry From the X-Intercepts"} :step/id #uuid "362cf052-a4cb-519a-968e-bda8b81a4551" :step/math-academy-id 502428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110352 :question/id #uuid "00575d99-28e6-59b7-b416-765808916fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14857"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23f4cc44-569e-56bd-aaa6-b72a10970679" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049244}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541127}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry From the Parabola’s Equation in Factored Form"} :step/id #uuid "f7dec3f3-b123-50ec-a737-75e54a076282" :step/math-academy-id 502432 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110353 :question/id #uuid "405e0230-69cb-5679-bec0-13a69c722934" :question/math-academy-id "e-14860"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89428829-1c13-5c7e-9fc2-ffe526a4c33a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541106}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value Given the Axis of Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "dd4bd192-4cdd-5f22-9524-55cd3e1d3ba8" :step/math-academy-id 502435 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111821 :tutorial/content "![](images/b2/b27906f92121928be444d3b7f616becbcc7a849e1313ef50accd8c8bfd718bfb.png)\n\nIf we know two points on a parabola with *identical* $y$-values, we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking an average of the two $x$-values. \n\nFor example, consider the parabola $y=f(x)$ above. Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=5$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(-3) = f(5) = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=-3$ and $x=5.$ We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabola's vertex is $(h,k),$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{-3+5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h=1$ is the $x$-coordinate of the vertex, and the axis of symmetry of the parabola is $x=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "23d4f6e0-c0a7-5551-bd88-80b3268a5b2b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13792 :tutorial/title "Extending the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1b132072-c297-50ce-be49-d3a763e0a32f" :step/math-academy-id 502439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110728 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110373 :question/id #uuid "04877936-271e-5fcb-a1ea-aa39c52482b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-19740"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a2eed2b-7f94-5b53-bc5c-4bf9aea18d32" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048398}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541071}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertex of a Parabola From Table Data"} :step/id #uuid "f7518575-386c-57e6-893c-6238e3cc01a1" :step/math-academy-id 502440}] :activity/title "The Average of the Roots Formula" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110824}]}
